-The eviction policy is still open. Candidates are LRU, frequency-based eviction with aging, and 2Q. LRU has little bookkeeping and follows recent work quickly but is vulnerable to sequential scans. Aged frequency counts protect repeatedly popular clips but add counter maintenance and can lag a project switch. 2Q separates first-use entries from reused entries, resisting scans at the cost of extra queues and a queue-size parameter. None changes the external interface, queue concurrency, or disk budget.
+### Eviction Policy: 2Q
+
+The cache uses a 2Q eviction policy with two queues:
+
+- **Am queue (first-use)**: Newly admitted entries are placed in the Am queue (FIFO). Size target: 25% of the cache budget (3 GiB). Entries that are never re-accessed age out here.
+- **A1 queue (reused)**: On a cache hit, the entry is promoted from Am to the head of A1 (LRU). Size target: 75% of the cache budget (9 GiB). Entries that are re-accessed stay in A1, ordered by recency of reuse.
+
+When admitting a completed preview, eviction proceeds in this order:
+1. Evict from the tail of Am until Am is within its size target.
+2. If Am is within target and A1 exceeds its target, evict from the tail of A1 until A1 is within its target.
+3. If both queues are within their targets, admit without eviction.
+
+The size targets (25%/75%) are the initial tuning parameters; they may be adjusted based on pilot workloads without changing the external interface.
+
+## Decisions & Alternatives
+
+| Decision | Options Considered | Rationale |
+|----------|-------------------|-----------|
+| Eviction policy | LRU, Frequency-based with aging, 2Q | 2Q protects repeatedly accessed comparison sheets from being evicted during long sequential screenings. The extra bookkeeping (two queues, promotion on hit) is accepted cost. LRU would evict comparison sheets during scans. Frequency-with-aging adds counter maintenance and can retain yesterday's favorites too long after a project switch. |
+| Queue split | 25%/75% (Am/A1), 50%/50%, 10%/90% | 25%/75% gives the working set (A1) the majority of space while keeping Am large enough to absorb screening scans without immediately promoting everything. 50%/50% wastes space on one-use entries. 10%/90% risks promoting entries too easily during a scan. |
